title: Log contexts
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Operations are grouped by nested log contexts (name, description, parent), created explicitly by scripts, commands and the GUI and passed along as an id — so leaving a context is just not passing it on.

A ''log context'' is `{id, name, description, parent, created}`. Each operation carries
its ''innermost'' context; the others are its ancestors, found by following the
parents. Same information as every context on every operation while contexts nest,
without copying three ids onto each of a bulk write's 50 000 operations. Ids start at
1: `0` is "no context".

Until November 2026 contexts were called ''labels'', and a command's label was opened
at its first write, found again by a key the client drew for the command
(`X-Metafolder-Command`), so that a command that only read opened nothing. That went
for explicit contexts (user decision, November 2026): a client creates its context
before it writes, and passes its id; there is no command key any more.

!! Passing a context

A context is ''not daemon state'':

* the daemon creates one on request, a child of the context the request names, and answers its id;
* every request that writes names its context in the header `X-Metafolder-Log-Context: <id>`; absent or `0`, no context. A request that does not write ignores it. A context that does not exist, or the watcher's or one inside it, is refused with `400`, and nothing is written;
* leaving a context is ceasing to pass it — the end of a subshell or a function's `local`. A script that crashes leaves nothing open behind it.

!! From the command line

`METAFOLDER_LOG_CONTEXT=<repo uuid>:<id>` is the current context of the `mf` commands; a
context id means nothing in another repository, so an `mf` sends it only with its
requests to that repository. Absent or `0`, there is none.

* `mf context new <name> [description]` creates a context, child of the current one, and prints it as `<repo uuid>:<id>`;
* `mf context parent` prints the parent of the current context, `0` at the root.

A script does

```
export METAFOLDER_LOG_CONTEXT=$(mf context new "tag run" "the 2024 photos")
```

— `export` each time: an `mf` inherits only what is exported, and the value holds only
for the script's shell and its children.

Every `mf` command that writes ''begins by creating its own context'', a child of the
current one, named after the command line and described by its query: the log shows
what was asked, not only what was written. A command that only reads creates none.

!! Who creates which

* A shipped tagging script: one context for the run, one per answer (its going back is reverting the last answer's context), one per command inside ([[Writing a GUI script]]).
* The GUI: one context per command run, created when the run begins; the `mf` object of the run carries it ([[Command transactions]]), so two commands running at once — a long reconcile and a field edited meanwhile — each have their own. A command run from inside another is in the caller's run. A request that names no run — a builtin's, the blocking client's, a click's — goes in the newest command's context, as all of them did before November 2026 (`crates/gui/src/daemon_proxy.rs`); the context is created at the run's first request that may write, so a command that only reads creates none. A script launched from the GUI (`script:run`) runs in a context of its own, passed on as `METAFOLDER_LOG_CONTEXT`; a plain `!` line is passed the work context alone (none without one), each `mf` it runs creating its own anyway.
* The GUI's ''work context'' ([[The GUI's work context]]): `context:set` creates a context on a repository that every later write the GUI sends there is inside until `context:unset` — naming the work under way, so it can be looked at or reverted whole. The runs' contexts are created inside it. The watcher's operations are not.
* The watcher: one context per flush ("watcher flush"), under a ''watcher root'' ("watcher") that only the daemon can write under: a client naming it, or a context inside it, as its context or as a new context's parent is refused. The ''origin'' stays on each operation, set by the daemon ([[Operations and their origin]]) — not read off the root: a trashing's `trash` origin must survive inside any context, and undo and retention read the operation.

!! Empty contexts

A context no operation and no child is in — never used, or emptied by retention or a
prune — stays until a reindex, which removes it (a reindex invalidates the work under
way anyway). Retention and prunes remove operations, never contexts
([[Automatic retention]], [[Pruning the log]]).

!! In the store

`log_contexts` (id → name · description · parent · created), `log_context_children`
(parent · child), `log_context_reverts` (what a context reverts and what reverts it)
([[Store tables]], [[Log context summaries]]).

!! The log panel

Operations grouped by their outermost context (name and description); a group opens
onto its child contexts, down to single operations. Consecutive watcher contexts are
folded into one line, or they would drown the rest.

!! Deferred

Renaming or describing a context after the fact.
